oduction processes are efficient and reliable, as outdated equipment may lead to increased variance and lower yield. The findings from this comparison can guide decisions on equipment upgrades and investments [Data: Entities (835, 836); Relationships (918)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Importance of Reaction Time in Yield Optimization\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Reaction time is a controllable variable that significantly influences the yield of chemical reactions. Chemical engineers manipulate this variable to optimize production outcomes. By conducting experiments that vary reaction time, they can identify optimal conditions that maximize yield. This process often involves statistical methods and experimental designs to ensure that the effects of reaction time are accurately assessed [Data: Entities (1612); Relationships (3199)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Impact of Reaction Temperature on Chemical Processes\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Similar to reaction time, reaction temperature is another critical factor that chemical engineers control to optimize yields. The relationship between temperature and yield is often modeled using statistical techniques, allowing engineers to identify the optimal temperature ranges for specific reactions. This understanding is vital for improving process efficiency and product quality, particularly in sensitive chemical syntheses [Data: Entities (1614); Relationships (3200)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Application of Statistical Methods in Chemical Engineering\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Chemical engineers frequently employ statistical methods, such as Design of Experiments (DOE) and Analysis of Variance (ANOVA), to analyze experimental data and optimize processes. These methods help in understanding the interactions between various factors, including reaction time and temperature, and their combined effects on yield. The use of statistical software tools enhances the ability to model complex relationships and make data-driven decisions [Data: Entities (1612, 1614); Relationships (3199, 3200)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Significance of Example 11.1 in Process Optimization\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Example 11.1 illustrates the application of a second-order model in optimizing chemical processes. This example demonstrates how polynomial models can capture the relationships between input variables and response variables, facilitating the identification of optimal conditions. The method of steepest ascent is highlighted as a practical approach for navigating the response surface towards optimal production conditions, showcasing the importance of mathematical modeling in chemical engineering [Data: Entities (2340); Relationships (3202)].\"\n        }\n    ]\n}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": [], "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null}, "content_filter_results": {"hate": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "protected_material_code": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "protected_material_text": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "self_harm": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "sexual": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "violence": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}}}], "created": 1768463306, "model": "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18", "object": "chat.completion", "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": "fp_f97eff32c5", "usage": {"completion_tokens": 794, "prompt_tokens": 5351, "total_tokens": 6145, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": 0, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cached_tokens": 0}}, "prompt_filter_results": [{"prompt_index": 0, "content_filter_results": {"hate": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "jailbreak": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "self_harm": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "sexual": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "violence": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}}}]}, "input": {"messages": [{"content": "\nYou are an AI assistant that helps a human analyst to perform general information discovery. Information discovery is the process of identifying and assessing relevant information associated with certain entities (e.g., organizations and individuals) within a network.\n\n# Goal\nWrite a comprehensive report of a community, given a list of entities that belong to the community as well as their relationships and optional associated claims. The report will be used to inform decision-makers about information associated with the community and their potential impact. The content of this report includes an overview of the community's key entities, their legal compliance, technical capabilities, reputation, and noteworthy claims.\n\n# Report Structure\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n834,CHEMICAL ENGINEER,\"**Entity: Chemical Engineer**\n\nA chemical engineer is a professional who applies principles of chemistry, biology, physics, mathematics, and engineering to solve problems related to the production or use of chemicals and materials. The primary purpose of a chemical engineer is to design and optimize processes that maximize the yield and efficiency of chemical production while ensuring safety and sustainability.\n\nIn terms of mathematical formulation, chemical engineers often utilize models that represent chemical processes, including reaction kinetics, thermodynamics, and mass and energy balances. These models can be expressed in various forms, such as differential equations that describe the rates of chemical reactions or algebraic equations that represent equilibrium conditions.\n\nThe design layout for a chemical engineering process typically involves several stages, including the conceptual design, detailed design, and operational phases. This may include the selection of appropriate equipment, the design of reactors, separation units, and heat exchangers, as well as the integration of control systems to monitor and optimize process conditions.\n\nKey assumptions required for the validity of chemical engineering analyses include the ideal behavior of gases and liquids, the applicability of thermodynamic principles, and the assumption of steady-state or dynamic conditions depending on the process being analyzed. \n\nChemical engineers are tasked with determining the optimal operating conditions for processes, which involves the use of statistical methods, including Design of Experiments (DOE) and Analysis of Variance (ANOVA), to analyze experimental data and identify significant factors affecting yield and quality. The analysis procedure typically involves conducting controlled experiments, collecting data, and applying statistical techniques to interpret the results.\n\nThe advantages of chemical engineering include the ability to innovate and improve processes, enhance product quality, and reduce costs through optimization. Chemical engineers play a crucial role in developing sustainable practices and minimizing environmental impact.\n\nHowever, there are limitations and disadvantages associated with chemical engineering, such as the complexity of chemical processes, the need for extensive safety measures, and the potential for high costs in research and development. Additionally, the assumptions made in modeling may not always hold true in real-world applications, leading to discrepancies between predicted and actual outcomes.\n\nRelated designs and methods include process optimization techniques, computational fluid dynamics (CFD) simulations, and various statistical quality control methods. Alternatives may involve using empirical models or heuristic approaches when traditional modeling is not feasible.\n\nPractical implementation considerations for chemical engineers include the need for interdisciplinary collaboration, adherence to safety regulations, and the integration of new technologies. The use of software tools for process simulation, statistical analysis, and optimization is common, with popular software packages including MATLAB, Aspen Plus, and Minitab.\n\nCommon applications of chemical engineering encompass a wide range of industries, including pharmaceuticals, petrochemicals, food processing, and materials science, where chemical engineers work to improve processes, develop new products, and ensure compliance with environmental standards.\",8\r\n835,OLD EQUIPMENT,The old equipment refers to type 1 test equipment suspected to have a larger variance in monitoring production processes.,2\r\n836,NEW EQUIPMENT,\"The new equipment refers to type 2 test equipment used to monitor production processes, which is being compared to the old equipment.\",2\r\n1612,REACTION TIME,\"**Comprehensive Summary of Reaction Time in Experimental Design**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nReaction time refers to the duration for which reactants are allowed to interact in a chemical process. It is a controllable variable that significantly influences the yield of a chemical reaction. In experimental designs, reaction time is often treated as a factor to assess its impact on the outcome of interest, such as the yield of a specific product, in this case, isatin.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nIn the context of a factorial design, the effect of reaction time can be modeled using a linear regression framework, where the yield (Y) can be expressed as a function of reaction time (T) and possibly other factors (X1, X2, ..., Xn):\n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 T + \\beta_2 X_1 + \\beta_3 X_2 + ... + \\beta_n X_n + \\epsilon \\]\nwhere \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_i \\) are the coefficients for each factor, and \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nIn experimental designs, reaction time can be incorporated into various layouts, such as full factorial designs, fractional factorial designs, or response surface methodologies. Each design allows for the systematic variation of reaction time alongside other factors to evaluate their combined effects on yield.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of conclusions drawn from experiments involving reaction time relies on several assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations: Each experimental run should be independent of others.\n2. Normality: The residuals from the model should be normally distributed.\n3. Homoscedasticity: The variance of residuals should be constant across all levels of reaction time.\n4. Linearity: The relationship between reaction time and yield should be linear or appropriately transformed.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nReaction time should be studied when it is hypothesized to influence the yield of a chemical process. It is particularly relevant in scenarios where optimizing yield is critical, such as in pharmaceutical synthesis or industrial chemical production. Selection criteria for including reaction time as a factor may include prior research indicating its significance, practical constraints on time, and the need for a comprehensive understanding of the reaction kinetics.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of the effect of reaction time on yield typically involves:\n1. Conducting the experiment according to the chosen design.\n2. Collecting yield data for each combination of factors.\n3. Performing ANOVA to determine the significance of reaction time and other factors.\n4. If significant, further analysis may include regression modeling to quantify the relationship.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Allows for the identification of optimal reaction times that maximize yield.\n- Facilitates understanding of the kinetics of the chemical process.\n- Can be integrated with other factors to provide a holistic view of the experimental system.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Requires careful control of other variables to isolate the effect of reaction time.\n- May involve significant time and resource investment, especially in lengthy reactions.\n- Results may be sensitive to the chosen levels of reaction time, necessitating thorough exploration of the design space.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated methodologies include response surface methodology (RSM) for optimizing multiple factors simultaneously, and time-series analysis for studying reaction kinetics over time. Alternatives may involve using fixed reaction times in a screening design to identify other significant factors.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing experiments involving reaction time, researchers should consider the feasibility of varying reaction times, the potential for time-dependent changes in reactant concentrations, and the need for precise timing mechanisms. Additionally, the experimental setup should allow for accurate measurement of yield at each time point.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nStatistical software such as R, SAS, or Minitab can be utilized for designing experiments, conducting ANOVA, and performing regression analysis to evaluate the effects of reaction time on yield.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nCommon applications of studying reaction time include pharmaceutical synthesis, where optimizing yield is crucial for cost-effectiveness, and in industrial chemical processes where reaction efficiency directly impacts production rates. For example, in the synthesis of isatin, varying the reaction time can lead to different yields, thus necessitating a thorough investigation of this factor.\",2\r\n1614,REACTION TEMPERATURE,\"**Comprehensive Summary of Reaction Temperature in Experimental Design**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nReaction temperature is a controllable variable in chemical processes that significantly influences the yield of a reaction. It is a critical factor in experimental designs aimed at optimizing chemical reactions, such as the synthesis of isatin, where understanding the effect of temperature on yield is essential for process efficiency and product quality.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nIn the context of a factorial design, the effect of reaction temperature can be modeled using a linear regression equation of the form:  \n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 T + \\epsilon \\]  \nwhere \\( Y \\) represents the yield, \\( T \\) is the reaction temperature, \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_1 \\) is the coefficient representing the effect of temperature on yield, and \\( \\epsilon \\) is the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nIn experimental designs, reaction temperature can be incorporated as a factor in various layouts, such as full factorial designs, fractional factorial designs, or response surface methodologies. Each design allows for the systematic investigation of the temperature's effect alongside other factors.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of the analysis involving reaction temperature relies on several assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations.\n2. Normality of residuals.\n3. Homogeneity of variance (homoscedasticity).\n4. Linearity of the relationship between temperature and yield.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nReaction temperature should be studied when it is hypothesized to have a significant effect on the yield of a chemical process. It is particularly applicable in scenarios where temperature variations are expected to influence reaction kinetics or thermodynamics. Selection criteria include the nature of the chemical reaction, the range of temperatures to be tested, and the desired precision of yield measurements.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of the effect of reaction temperature typically involves:\n1. Designing the experiment to include temperature as a factor.\n2. Conducting the experiment and collecting yield data.\n3. Performing ANOVA to assess the significance of temperature on yield.\n4. If significant, further analysis may include regression modeling to quantify the relationship.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nStudying reaction temperature allows for:\n- Optimization of yield, leading to increased efficiency in chemical processes.\n- Identification of optimal temperature ranges for desired product formation.\n- Enhanced understanding of reaction mechanisms.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nLimitations include:\n- Potential for confounding effects if not properly controlled.\n- The need for precise temperature control, which may require specialized equipment.\n- The possibility of non-linear effects that may not be captured by simple linear models.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated experimental designs include:\n- Response Surface Methodology (RSM) for exploring the interaction between temperature and other factors.\n- Central Composite Designs (CCD) for optimizing multiple factors, including temperature.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing studies involving reaction temperature, researchers should consider:\n- The range of temperatures to be tested and their relevance to the chemical process.\n- The need for replicates to ensure statistical validity.\n- The calibration and maintenance of temperature control equipment.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nStatistical software such as R, SAS, or Minitab can be utilized for designing experiments, conducting ANOVA, and performing regression analysis to evaluate the effect of reaction temperature on yield.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nCommon applications of studying reaction temperature include:\n- The synthesis of pharmaceuticals, where temperature optimization can lead to higher yields and purities.\n- Industrial chemical processes, such as polymerization or fermentation, where temperature control is crucial for product consistency.\n\nIn summary, reaction temperature is a vital factor in the design of experiments aimed at optimizing chemical yields, particularly in processes like the synthesis of isatin. Its careful study can lead to significant improvements in efficiency and product quality.\",2\r\n2340,EXAMPLE 11.1,\"**Summary of Example 11.1: Second-Order Model and Method of Steepest Ascent in Chemical Process Optimization**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nExample 11.1 presents a second-order model involving two variables, \\( x \\) and \\( y \\), which is utilized to analyze and optimize a chemical process. The purpose of this example is to demonstrate how a second-order polynomial can effectively capture the relationship between the input variables and the response variable, facilitating the identification of optimal conditions for the chemical process.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nThe second-order model can be mathematically expressed as:\n\\[\nY = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 y + \\beta_{11} x^2 + \\beta_{22} y^2 + \\beta_{12} xy + \\epsilon\n\\]\nwhere \\( Y \\) is the response variable, \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_1, \\beta_2 \\) are the linear coefficients, \\( \\beta_{11}, \\beta_{22}, \\beta_{12} \\) are the quadratic and interaction coefficients, and \\( \\epsilon \\) represents the error term.\n\n**Design Layout:**\nThe design layout for this example typically involves a factorial or central composite design that allows for the estimation of the coefficients in the second-order model. The design should include a sufficient number of runs to capture the curvature of the response surface.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of the second-order model relies on several assumptions:\n1. The relationship between the response and predictor variables is adequately captured by the polynomial form.\n2. The errors are normally distributed with constant variance (homoscedasticity).\n3. The observations are independent.\n4. The model is correctly specified, meaning all relevant variables are included.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nThis model is applicable when the researcher suspects a nonlinear relationship between the input variables and the response. It is particularly useful in optimization scenarios where the goal is to find the maximum or minimum of a response variable based on multiple factors.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis typically involves fitting the second-order model to the experimental data using regression techniques. The method of steepest ascent can then be applied to navigate the response surface towards the optimal conditions. This involves calculating the gradient of the response surface and moving in the direction of the steepest increase.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- The second-order model provides a flexible framework for capturing nonlinear relationships.\n- It allows for the identification of interaction effects between variables.\n- The method of steepest ascent is straightforward and intuitive for optimization.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- The model may become complex with an increasing number of variables, leading to difficulties in interpretation.\n- Overfitting can occur if the model is too complex relative to the amount of data available.\n- The method of steepest ascent may not always lead to a global optimum, especially in the presence of multiple local optima.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated designs include response surface methodology (RSM) and Box-Behnken designs, which also focus on optimizing responses based on multiple factors. Alternatives to the method of steepest ascent include the use of gradient descent or other optimization algorithms.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing this model, it is crucial to ensure that the experimental design is robust enough to capture the necessary data points for accurate model fitting. Additionally, careful consideration should be given to the selection of the starting point for the steepest ascent method to avoid local optima.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nStatistical software packages such as R, SAS, and Minitab can be utilized to fit the second-order model and perform the necessary analyses. These tools often include built-in functions for regression analysis and optimization techniques.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nExample 11.1 is relevant in various fields, particularly in chemical engineering, where optimizing reaction conditions is critical. It can also be applied in agricultural experiments to optimize fertilizer application rates or in manufacturing processes to enhance product quality.\",3\r\n2341,TABLE 11.1,Table 11.1 presents process data for fitting the first-order model in the context of the chemical engineering example.,1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n916,CHEMICAL ENGINEER,OLD EQUIPMENT,The chemical engineer is investigating the performance and variance of the old equipment in comparison to the new equipment,10\r\n917,CHEMICAL ENGINEER,NEW EQUIPMENT,The chemical engineer is investigating the performance and variance of the new equipment in comparison to the old equipment,10\r\n3199,CHEMICAL ENGINEER,REACTION TIME,The chemical engineer controls reaction time to optimize the yield of the process,10\r\n3200,CHEMICAL ENGINEER,REACTION TEMPERATURE,The chemical engineer controls reaction temperature to optimize the yield of the process,10\r\n918,OLD EQUIPMENT,NEW EQUIPMENT,The old equipment is being compared to the new equipment to determine which has a larger variance,4\r\n3202,EXAMPLE 11.1,TABLE 11.1,Table 11.1 provides data relevant to the example discussed in Example 11.1,4\r\n\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the communi